@article{ WOS:001381964800001,
Author = {Acebes, P. and Munoz-Galvez, F. J. and Iglesias-Gonzalez, Z. and
   Garcia-Llorente, M. and Lopez-Garcia, D.},
Title = {Unveiling human-wildlife interactions in the context of livestock
   grazing abandonment and the return of large carnivores, ungulates and
   vultures: A stakeholder perspective},
Journal = {PEOPLE AND NATURE},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {8},
Number = {7},
Pages = {2078-2090},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {Pastoral practices remain a widespread economic activity across European
   mountain regions. However, the viability of this activity may be
   threatened by the recovery of large wild vertebrates associated with
   passive rewilding, leading to the so-called human-wildlife conflicts.
   Reconciling the return of large wild vertebrates and pastoral practices
   is therefore a major challenge for the European Union (EU), as it poses
   political, socio-economic and conservation challenges. We analyse the
   social dimension of the recovery of large carnivores, ungulates and
   strict avian scavengers, through a multi-stakeholder approach in two
   rural territories in central Spain exposed to different degrees of land
   abandonment. Based on in-depth interviews, we identify potential
   conflicts and disagreements between the different stakeholders, search
   for strategies to promote the coexistence of large wild vertebrates and
   extensive grazing systems and seek emergent discourses for reconciling
   and boosting agrarian production and biodiversity conservation. Our
   findings show a consensus among stakeholders on the general increase and
   recovery of large wild vertebrate populations. We then collected
   divergent positions on what this recovery means for social groups,
   showing opposing views between urban and rural people; while
   conservationists considered this increase positively, land users
   generally-but not always-perceived this recovery as a threat to their
   livelihoods. We identified disagreements within social groups and
   divergent positions displayed between cattle farmers and sheep and goat
   farmers. The latter showed forms of management closer to agroecological
   visions, thus allowing coexistence. The coexistence of pastoral
   practices and wild vertebrates involves navigating a complex interplay
   of ecological, social and economic factors. The negative impacts of
   wildlife on livestock can be minimized, but only through the collective
   implementation of measures by all parties. Furthermore, the recognition
   and valuation of pastoral practices and the empowerment of land users
   may facilitate coexistence. Mutual recognition and participation of
   local stakeholders seems an unavoidable step in developing more
   collaborative approaches that respect the needs and aspirations of rural
   communities while contributing to the EU's restoration and conservation
   goals.Read the free Plain Language Summary for this article on the
   Journal blog.
   Las pr \& aacute;cticas de pastoreo contin \& uacute;an siendo una
   actividad econ \& oacute;mica clave en las regiones monta \& ntilde;osas
   de Europa. Sin embargo, la recuperaci \& oacute;n de grandes vertebrados
   silvestres, vinculada a la llamada ``renaturalizaci \& oacute;n
   pasiva{''} (del ingl \& eacute;s passive rewilding), plantea nuevos
   desaf \& iacute;os que pueden amenazar la viabilidad de esta actividad.
   Estos desaf \& iacute;os, conocidos como conflictos entre humanos y
   fauna silvestre, suponen un reto significativo para la Uni \& oacute;n
   Europea (UE), al generar tensiones pol \& iacute;ticas, socioecon \&
   oacute;micas y de conservaci \& oacute;n. En este estudio, exploramos la
   dimensi \& oacute;n social de la recuperaci \& oacute;n de grandes carn
   \& iacute;voros, ungulados y aves carro \& ntilde;eras mediante un
   enfoque multiactor en dos territorios rurales del centro de Espa \&
   ntilde;a con distintos grados de abandono de las pr \& aacute;cticas
   pastorales. A trav \& eacute;s de entrevistas en profundidad,
   identificamos conflictos y desacuerdos potenciales entre los actores
   implicados, evaluamos estrategias para fomentar la coexistencia entre
   grandes vertebrados silvestres y sistemas ganaderos extensivos, y
   analizamos discursos emergentes que buscan equilibrar la producci \&
   oacute;n agraria sostenible con la conservaci \& oacute;n de la
   biodiversidad. Nuestros resultados reflejan un amplio consenso entre los
   actores sobre el aumento y recuperaci \& oacute;n de las poblaciones de
   grandes vertebrados silvestres. Sin embargo, se observaron posturas
   divergentes respecto a lo que esta recuperaci \& oacute;n significa para
   los distintos grupos sociales. Mientras los conservacionistas valoran
   positivamente este fen \& oacute;meno, los usuarios de la tierra-aunque
   no de manera uniforme-lo perciben mayoritariamente como una amenaza para
   sus medios de vida. Adem \& aacute;s, identificamos desacuerdos y
   posiciones divergentes dentro de los grupos de actores, especialmente
   entre los ganaderos de vacuno y los de peque \& ntilde;os rumiantes
   (ovino y caprino). Estos \& uacute;ltimos adoptan pr \& aacute;cticas de
   manejo m \& aacute;s alineadas con visiones agroecol \& oacute;gicas,
   favoreciendo as \& iacute; una mayor coexistencia con la fauna
   silvestre. La coexistencia entre la ganader \& iacute;a extensiva y las
   poblaciones de grandes vertebrados requiere abordar una compleja
   interacci \& oacute;n de factores ecol \& oacute;gicos, sociales y econ
   \& oacute;micos. Los impactos negativos de la fauna silvestre sobre el
   ganado pueden mitigarse, pero solo mediante la implementaci \& oacute;n
   colectiva de medidas concertadas entre todos los actores implicados.
   Asimismo, el reconocimiento y la valorizaci \& oacute;n-tanto
   sociocultural como econ \& oacute;mica-de las pr \& aacute;cticas
   pastorales, junto con la capacitaci \& oacute;n del sector agrario,
   resultan esenciales para facilitar esta coexistencia. El reconocimiento
   mutuo y la participaci \& oacute;n activa de los actores locales se
   perfilan como pasos imprescindibles para desarrollar enfoques
   colaborativos que respeten las necesidades y aspiraciones de las
   comunidades rurales, al tiempo que contribuyen a los objetivos de
   restauraci \& oacute;n y conservaci \& oacute;n de la UE.},
